<div class="article-body">
    <div class="callout">
      <h2 id="glance" style="margin-top:0">At a glance</h2>
      <ol class="stop-steps">
        <li><strong>Passive hang (dead hang):</strong> arms straight, shoulders relaxed and rising toward your ears.</li>
        <li><strong>Active hang:</strong> arms straight, shoulder blades pulled down and back, body a few centimeters higher.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick passive</strong> for grip endurance and a relaxed stretch. <strong>Pick active</strong> for shoulder blade control and pull-up preparation.</li>
        <li><strong>Typical holds:</strong> 20 to 30 seconds passive and 15 to 20 seconds active. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</li>
        <li><strong>Loose shoulders or past dislocations:</strong> ask a clinician first. The <a href="/deadhang-safety/#ask-first">safety page</a> explains why.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>What Is the Difference Between an Active and a Passive Hang?</h2>
      <p>A passive hang, also called a dead hang, lets the shoulders relax so they rise toward the ears. An active hang pulls the shoulder blades down a few centimeters while the arms stay straight. Both use the same grip and the fingers carry your bodyweight in both. The shoulder muscles work more in an active hang.</p>
      <div class="visual-block">
        <div class="dg-panels" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));max-width:440px" role="img" aria-label="Passive hang with relaxed shoulders next to active hang with shoulder blades pulled down">
<div class="hf-card"><p class="hf-card-title">Passive hang</p>${hangFigureSvg({ pose: 'dead-hang', hl: 'shoulders' })}<p class="hf-card-lines"><span>Shoulders relaxed</span><span>Rise toward ears</span></p></div>
<div class="hf-card"><p class="hf-card-title">Active hang</p>${hangFigureSvg({ pose: 'active', hl: 'shoulders' })}<p class="hf-card-lines"><span>Shoulder blades down</span><span>Arms still straight</span></p></div>
</div>
        <p class="caption">Same bar, same straight arms. Only the shoulder blades change.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Factor</th>
            <th scope="col">Passive (dead) hang</th>
            <th scope="col">Active hang</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Shoulder position</strong></td>
            <td>Relaxed, rising toward the ears</td>
            <td>Shoulder blades held down and back</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Arms</strong></td>
            <td>Straight</td>
            <td>Straight</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Grip demand</strong></td>
            <td>High</td>
            <td>High; shoulder muscles work in addition</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Primary muscles</strong></td>
            <td>Forearm flexors and stretched lats</td>
            <td>Lower traps, serratus anterior and working lats</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Typical hold</strong></td>
            <td>20 to 30 seconds</td>
            <td>15 to 20 seconds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Difficulty</strong></td>
            <td>Beginner-friendly</td>
            <td>Intermediate: needs body awareness</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Best for</strong></td>
            <td>Stretching, relaxed holds, grip endurance</td>
            <td>Shoulder blade control and pull-up preparation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Rehab use</strong></td>
            <td>Used informally; not shown in trials</td>
            <td>Used informally; not shown in trials</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>The hold times are common training practice and not a tested prescription. Both versions share the same <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">overhand grip setup</a>.</p>

      <h2>What Is a Passive Hang?</h2>
      <p>A passive hang is a dead hang with relaxed shoulders. You grip the bar, let your bodyweight stretch the arms and shoulders, and the shoulder blades slide up toward your ears. Only the forearms work to keep hold. Many people use it as a stretch after sitting or lifting, and as a grip endurance test.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Grip the bar overhand at shoulder width and step off with straight arms.</li>
        <li>Relax every muscle except your forearms and let your shoulders rise toward your ears.</li>
        <li>Breathe slowly and hold for 20 to 30 seconds.</li>
        <li>Step down under control before your grip gives out.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>In any straight-arm hang the fingers support your bodyweight, so the flexor digitorum profundus and superficialis keep your fingers closed around the bar. Passive hangs let you hold longer than active hangs because the shoulder muscles work less. The lats, pecs and teres major stretch through their overhead range.</p>
      <p>Hanging unloads the spine while you hang. Its effect on discs has not been measured in the sources cited on this site. See the <a href="/spine-decompression/">spine decompression guide</a> for the evidence.</p>

      <h2>What Is an Active Hang?</h2>
      <p>An active hang is a dead hang with the shoulder blades pulled down and slightly together while the arms stay straight. The body rises a few centimeters, and the lower trapezius, serratus anterior and lats work to hold the position. It practices the shoulder blade position used at the start of a pull-up.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Grip the bar and hang with straight arms in a passive position.</li>
        <li>Pull your shoulder blades down toward your back pockets without bending your elbows.</li>
        <li>Hold the position for 15 to 20 seconds and keep breathing.</li>
        <li>Release back to a passive hang before you step down.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="visual-block">
        ${demoLoopHtml('passive-to-active', { caption: 'Passive hang to active hang: the shoulder blades pull down and the chest lifts.' })}
      </div>
      <p>The muscular demand is higher, so your times will be shorter than passive hangs. Upward drift of the shoulders means you have lost the active position. Bending the elbows turns the hold into the start of a pull-up. Repeating the shoulder blade movement up and down is a scapular pull-up, which is a different exercise. Read the <a href="/shoulder-health/">shoulder health guide</a> for more on shoulder programming.</p>

      <h2>Should Your Shoulders Be by Your Ears in a Dead Hang?</h2>
      <p>In a passive dead hang, yes. The shoulders are relaxed, so your bodyweight lifts them toward your ears while the bar holds you up. In an active hang they sit a few centimeters lower because the shoulder blades are pulled down. If the passive position feels pinching, loose or unstable, step down and ask a clinician before continuing.</p>
      <p>Many beginners do not fully relax during passive hangs. Their shoulders stay partly engaged out of habit or fear, which gives a hybrid position that is neither a full passive stretch nor a clear active hang. Shrug your shoulders toward your ears once after gripping the bar, then let them settle. Your arms should feel like ropes connecting your hands to your torso.</p>

      <h2>Active or Passive Hang: Which Should You Use?</h2>
      <p>Choose a passive hang for grip endurance and a relaxed stretch, and an active hang for shoulder blade control and pull-up preparation. Anyone with loose shoulders, past dislocations or shoulder pain should ask a clinician before hanging, because neither version has been tested in those groups.</p>
      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable decision table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Your situation</th>
            <th scope="col">Start with</th>
            <th scope="col">Note</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Longer holds or grip endurance</strong></td>
            <td>Passive</td>
            <td>The shoulder muscles work less, so your fingers become the limit.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Stretch after sitting or lifting</strong></td>
            <td>Passive</td>
            <td>Short holds of 20 to 30 seconds are common. Ask a clinician first with back pain or a diagnosed spine condition.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Pull-up preparation or shoulder blade control</strong></td>
            <td>Active</td>
            <td>It practices the shoulder blade position that starts a pull-up. Use a passive hang to warm up.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Loose or hypermobile shoulders, or past dislocations</strong></td>
            <td>Ask a clinician first</td>
            <td>Physiotherapy academics advise ten seconds or fewer to start. Some people with loose shoulders keep the shoulder blades engaged. No trial has compared the two.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Shoulder pain</strong></td>
            <td>Ask a clinician first</td>
            <td>Stop if symptoms increase. Some physiotherapists use active hangs in shoulder rehab, but trials have not tested them.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Cannot hold 5 seconds yet</strong></td>
            <td>Neither yet</td>
            <td>Build a base with feet-supported hangs first. A <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorway pull-up bar</a> makes short daily practice easy to fit in.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>The physiotherapy advice comes from <a href="https://theconversation.com/what-are-dead-hangs-what-are-the-shoulder-pain-risks-and-how-do-i-do-them-safely-202346">The Conversation (21 April 2023)</a>. It is expert opinion and not a trial. Some climbers use active hangs as part of a warm-up. No study shows that this reduces injury.</p>

      <h2>How to Program Active and Passive Hangs Together</h2>
      <p>In a session made only of hangs, start with passive hangs and finish with active hangs. Before an upper body session, an active hang can open the workout and a passive hang can follow it. Track hold times for each version separately, because the two hangs are different tests.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Sample Combined Protocol</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Passive hangs:</strong> 3 sets of 20 to 30 seconds. Rest 60 seconds between sets.</li>
          <li><strong>Active hangs:</strong> 3 sets of 15 to 20 seconds. Rest 60 seconds between sets.</li>
          <li><strong>Frequency:</strong> Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days. Add a session only if you stay pain free.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The sample protocol is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Alternate emphasis between sessions when time is limited. One session leads with passive hangs and adds one set of active hangs. The next leads with active hangs and adds one set of passive hangs. Follow the <a href="/deadhang-progressions/">dead hang progression ladder</a> to advance both versions step by step.</p>

      <h2>Common Active and Passive Hang Mistakes</h2>
      <p>Three active and passive hang mistakes to check first are half-engaging the shoulder blades, bending the elbows and holding your breath. Each one blurs the difference between the two hangs or shortens your hold. Review <a href="/deadhang-form/">form and breathing technique</a> for complete guidance.</p>
      <h3>Not Engaging Fully During Active Hangs</h3>
      <p>Partial shoulder blade depression looks correct from the outside but recruits the lower trapezius less. Your body rises only slightly instead of a few centimeters. Imagine you push the bar away from your body while pulling your shoulder blades toward your back pockets. The contraction should feel clear across the mid-back.</p>
      <h3>Bending the Elbows</h3>
      <p>Elbow bend turns an active hang into a partial pull-up. Keep your arms straight in both versions so the shoulder joint and forearms do the work without biceps assistance.</p>
      <h3>Holding Your Breath</h3>
      <p>Breathe continuously during both versions. Do not hold your breath.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>What is the difference between a dead hang and an active hang?</h3>
        <p>A dead hang keeps your shoulders relaxed so they rise toward your ears. An active hang pulls the shoulder blades down and back. Both use the same overhand bar grip and the fingers support your bodyweight in both. The shoulder muscles work more in an active hang.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Is a dead hang the same as a passive hang?</h3>
        <p>Yes. A dead hang and a passive hang are the same exercise: straight arms, relaxed shoulders and your full bodyweight on the bar. The term active hang names the version with the shoulder blades pulled down. Some writers use dead hang for both, so check which shoulder position a page describes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Should your shoulders be up by your ears in a dead hang?</h3>
        <p>In a passive dead hang, yes. The shoulders are relaxed, so they rise toward your ears while the bar holds your weight. In an active hang they sit a few centimeters lower. If the passive position feels pinching, loose or unstable, step down and ask a clinician before continuing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Should a hypermobile beginner use active or passive hangs?</h3>
        <p>Ask a physical therapist or doctor first. Two physiotherapy academics writing in The Conversation say dead hangs are not the best exercise for people with shoulder hypermobility or instability. Some people with loose shoulders keep the shoulder blades engaged, but no trial has compared active and passive hangs in hypermobile people.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Should I do passive or active hangs first?</h3>
        <p>In a session made only of hangs, start with passive hangs to stretch the shoulders. Finish with active hangs to practice shoulder blade control while the joints are warm. Before an upper body workout, an active hang can open the session. A passive hang can follow it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Can I do both passive and active hangs in the same workout?</h3>
        <p>Yes. A common plan for one workout is 2 to 3 sets of passive hangs for 20 to 30 seconds each. Then do 2 to 3 sets of active hangs for 15 to 20 seconds each. Rest 60 seconds between sets. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
      </div>

